        <div id="why-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Choose a Gas Snow Blower Over Battery or Corded
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gas snow blower is still the machine to beat when the snow is deep, wet or frequent. Battery models have improved quickly, but gas engines keep two advantages that matter most in a hard winter: sustained power and unlimited run time. If you are still deciding between the two, our <a href="/electric-vs-gas-snow-blower/">electric vs gas snow blower comparison</a> walks through the full trade-off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power That Does Not Fade</h3>

          <p>
            A gas engine produces the same power at the end of a two-hour clearing session as at the start. That matters for the heavy, wet snow that falls near freezing and for the dense ridge a city plow leaves across the end of the driveway, which is often the hardest snow you will move all winter. Engines from 200cc to over 300cc drive two-stage systems that chew through that kind of snow where many electric machines slow down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run Time Is a Fuel Can Away</h3>

          <p>
            When a battery runs flat, you stop and recharge. When a gas tank runs low, you refill it from a can and keep going. For long rural driveways, corner lots with long sidewalks, or anyone who clears a neighbor's drive too, that alone decides it. Keep a dedicated, clearly labeled can of fresh fuel for the machine; our <a href="/best-gas-can/">best gas cans guide</a> covers compliant cans that pour without spilling into a small tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Electric Makes More Sense</h3>

          <p>
            For a short paved driveway, a deck or sidewalks that get a few inches at a time, a battery or corded machine is lighter, quieter and needs no fuel, oil changes or off-season draining. If that describes your winters, our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">best electric snow blowers guide</a> is the better place to start. Gas pays off as the snow gets deeper and the driveway gets longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Single-Stage or Two-Stage Gas Snow Blower? Let Your Snow and Surface Decide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every gas snow blower is either single-stage or two-stage, and that one choice determines what snow it can handle, what surfaces it can work on and how big it is to store. Get this right and the rest of the decision is about size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single-Stage: Light, Nimble, Pavement Only</h3>

          <p>
            A single-stage machine uses one fast-spinning auger with rubber paddles to scoop snow and throw it out of the chute in a single motion. The paddles touch the ground, so they scrape the pavement clean and help pull the machine forward. Single-stage gas blowers are lighter and easier to store, and they handle up to roughly a foot of fresh snow on paved surfaces. They should not be used on gravel, because the paddles pick up stones and throw them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Stage: The Workhorse for Real Winters</h3>

          <p>
            A two-stage machine splits the job. A slower steel auger breaks up the snow and feeds it to a separate high-speed impeller, which throws it out of the chute. The housing rides on skid shoes above the surface, so it works on gravel when the shoes are set high, and the steel auger handles packed, icy and wet snow. Two-stage machines are self-propelled with multiple speeds, which matters because they are heavy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three-Stage Machines</h3>

          <p>
            Some brands sell three-stage machines that add an accelerator in front of the impeller. They are aimed at very heavy snow and are larger and costlier. For most homes, a well-sized two-stage machine does the job, which is why every pick here except one is two-stage.
          </p>

