        <div id="engine-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Engine Size: What 200cc, 250cc and 300cc Actually Buy You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Engine displacement, measured in cubic centimeters, is the simplest guide to how hard a gas snow blower can work. More cc means more torque to keep the auger and impeller turning in heavy snow, but it also means more weight and more fuel burned per hour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Around 200 to 225cc</h3>

          <p>
            Engines in this range, like the 212cc PowerSmart and the 223cc Briggs & Stratton machines, suit 22- to 26-inch housings and ordinary snowfalls of up to about a foot. They start easily and are fuel-efficient. In a single-stage machine, a 223cc engine is generous; in a 26-inch two-stage, it is adequate for most suburban driveways.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Around 250cc</h3>

          <p>
            The Ariens Deluxe 24 (254cc) and Husqvarna ST227 (252cc) sit here. The extra displacement helps a machine hold its speed through heavy, wet snow and the plow ridge rather than bogging down, which is why Ariens puts a bigger engine in a 24-inch housing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">300cc and Up</h3>

          <p>
            The Champion (301cc) and the 30-inch Briggs & Stratton (306cc) are built for wide housings and deep snow. If you regularly get storms of more than a foot, live where lake-effect snow piles up, or clear a long driveway, this class saves the most time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Torque Ratings</h3>

          <p>
            Briggs & Stratton also lists gross torque in foot-pounds, 10.0 for its 223cc engine and 14.5 for its 306cc engine. Torque is a useful comparison within one brand, but different makers measure differently, so compare cc first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="width-intake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Clearing Width and Intake Height: Size It to Your Driveway
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clearing width decides how many passes you need, and intake height decides how deep a drift the machine can take in one bite. Match both to your driveway and your typical storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One-Car Driveways and Walks</h3>

          <p>
            A 22- to 24-inch machine is enough for a single-car driveway and front walk. It is easier to steer around cars and store in a crowded garage. The 22-inch single-stage Briggs suits paved drives with moderate snow; the 24-inch PowerSmart or Ariens suit heavier snow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Car Driveways</h3>

          <p>
            A 26- to 27-inch machine is the sweet spot for most two-car driveways. It clears noticeably more per pass than a 24-inch housing without becoming hard to handle. The Briggs 26-inch, Champion 27-inch and Husqvarna ST227 all fit here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Long, Wide or Shared Driveways</h3>

          <p>
            For three-car driveways, long rural drives or a cul-de-sac you share with neighbors, a 30-inch machine like the larger Briggs & Stratton saves the most time. Check that you have the garage space and the strength to maneuver it, ideally with power steering.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Intake Height</h3>

          <p>
            Intake height is the height of the housing opening. Most two-stage machines here list 19 to 21 inches, while the single-stage Briggs lists clearing up to 12 inches deep. If drifts regularly reach your knees, look for 21 inches; snow taller than the intake spills over and has to be cleared in layers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electric-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Electric Start on a Gas Snow Blower: Worth Having
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pick in this guide has electric start, and on a gas snow blower that is close to essential. Cold engines are hard to pull-start, and the coldest mornings are exactly when you need the machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How 120-Volt Plug-In Start Works</h3>

          <p>
            Most gas snow blowers use a 120-volt starter: you plug an extension cord from a household outlet into the engine, press the button, and the starter spins the engine until it fires. Then you unplug and go. The machine has no battery to maintain, but you do need an outlet within reach of where you start it, usually in the garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Recoil as Backup</h3>

          <p>
            Electric-start machines still have a recoil pull cord, as the Briggs & Stratton listings note. If you are out on the driveway and the engine stalls, you can restart it with the cord rather than walking back to an outlet. Briggs lists starting down to minus 20 degrees Fahrenheit for its single-stage model.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Starting Tips for Cold Mornings</h3>

          <p>
            Follow the maker's starting sequence: fuel valve on, choke set, primer pressed the listed number of times, then start. Start the engine in a ventilated space with the door open, never in a closed garage, and let it warm up briefly before engaging the auger.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gas in a Snow Blower: Fresh Fuel, Stabilizer and Ethanol
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most gas snow blowers that will not start have a fuel problem, not an engine problem. Small engines are sensitive to stale fuel, and a snow blower sits unused for months. Getting fuel right is the cheapest reliability upgrade there is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use Fresh Regular Gasoline</h3>

          <p>
            Most snow blower engines run on regular unleaded gasoline; check your manual for the minimum octane and maximum ethanol content the maker allows. Buy only as much as you expect to use in about a month, and fill the machine from a clean can. Gasoline starts to degrade within a few months, and stale fuel leaves varnish that clogs the small passages in a carburetor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ethanol: Why It Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Most pump gas contains up to 10 percent ethanol. Ethanol attracts water and can separate from the gasoline over time, which causes hard starting and corrosion. Never use fuel with more ethanol than your manual allows. Many owners pay extra for ethanol-free fuel or canned small-engine fuel for their snow blower, especially for the last tank of the season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add Stabilizer When You Fill the Can</h3>

          <p>
            Add fuel stabilizer to the can at the pump, not weeks later, so every tank you pour is treated. Run the engine for a few minutes after adding treated fuel so it reaches the carburetor. Our <a href="/best-fuel-stabilizer/">fuel stabilizer guide</a> covers products and dosing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Oil Too</h3>

          <p>
            Gas snow blowers have four-stroke engines with a separate oil reservoir, so you never mix oil into the fuel. Check the oil level before each storm and change it as the manual schedules, using the cold-weather oil grade the maker specifies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drive-steering" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Self-Propelled Drive, Speeds and Steering
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two-stage gas machines are heavy, so the drive system decides how tiring they are to use. Our <a href="/self-propelled-snow-blower/">self-propelled snow blower guide</a> goes deeper, but here is what to look for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Forward and Reverse Speeds</h3>

          <p>
            Six forward and two reverse speeds, as most picks here list, let you crawl through heavy snow and walk briskly on the return pass. Reverse speeds help you back out of a tight spot without dragging a heavy machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power Steering</h3>

          <p>
            Trigger power steering, listed on the Husqvarna ST227 and the 30-inch Briggs, disengages one wheel so the machine pivots easily. It matters most on 27- and 30-inch machines. Ariens' Auto-Turn steering does the same job without triggers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tires and Traction</h3>

          <p>
            Larger, deep-tread tires grip better on packed snow and slopes. The 30-inch Briggs lists 16-inch snow tires and the Husqvarna lists locking X-trac tires. On a steep driveway, traction matters as much as engine size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Gas Snow Blower Safety: The Rules That Prevent Injuries
          </h2>

          <p>
            Snow blowers cause serious hand injuries every winter, almost always from the same mistake. Read the operator's manual and follow the maker's instructions and safety warnings. These rules matter most.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Clear a Clogged Chute by Hand</h3>

          <p>
            Shut the engine off, wait for all moving parts to stop, and use a clearing tool or a stick, never your hand. The impeller can hold stored energy and turn after the engine stops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carbon Monoxide</h3>

          <p>
            A gas engine produces carbon monoxide. Never run a snow blower in a closed garage or shed, even briefly to warm it up. Start it with the door open and move it outside promptly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refuel Cold, Outdoors</h3>

          <p>
            Refuel only when the engine is off and cool, outdoors and away from flames or sparks. Spilled gasoline on a hot engine can ignite. Wipe up spills and close the fuel cap before restarting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear the Area</h3>

          <p>
            Before each pass, look for newspapers, toys, doormats and hoses buried in the snow, and keep people and pets well away from the chute. A two-stage machine can throw objects with real force.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Storing a Gas Snow Blower Between Storms and Over Summer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Storage habits decide whether the machine starts next December. Our <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-snow-blower-for-winter/">guide to preparing a snow blower for winter</a> covers the fall start-up checklist; here is the storage side.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Between Storms</h3>

          <p>
            After each use, let the engine run for a minute with the auger engaged to clear slush, then brush the snow off the housing, chute and controls so they do not freeze solid. Store it in a garage or shed with stabilized fuel in the tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">End of Season: Fuel</h3>

          <p>
            At the end of winter, either run the tank dry or fill it with stabilized fuel and run the engine to circulate it, depending on what your manual recommends. Never leave untreated fuel in the tank over summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">End of Season: Engine and Machine</h3>

          <p>
            Change the oil, check the spark plug, inspect the shear pins, belts, skid shoes and paddles, and lubricate the parts the manual lists. Store the machine covered, away from appliances with a pilot light, and fold the handle if it folds, as the single-stage Briggs & Stratton's handle does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Space in the Garage</h3>

          <p>
            Measure before you buy. A 30-inch two-stage machine takes up a lot of floor space and is heavy to push around when the engine is off, so plan where it will live.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="toro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Toro Gas Snow Blowers and Where to Buy Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Toro is one of the most searched gas snow blower brands, and its Power Clear single-stage and Power Max two-stage machines have a strong reputation. They are not in this ranking for a simple reason: when we searched Amazon for this guide, new Toro gas snow blowers were not listed there, only replacement parts and covers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Toro Gas Models Are Sold</h3>

          <p>
            Toro sells its gas snow blowers mainly through hardware stores, home centers and authorized dealers. A dealer is worth considering for a large two-stage machine anyway, because it can assemble it, set it up and handle warranty service.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Toro Compares</h3>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-snow-blower/">best snow blowers guide</a> covers the Toro Power Max 826 OHAE, Toro SnowMaster 824 QXE and Toro Power Clear 721 R-C alongside battery and Honda models. The picks here are the gas models you can order on Amazon.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Comparing Across Brands</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever the brand, compare the same specs: single- or two-stage, engine cc, clearing width, intake height, electric start, drive speeds and warranty. Those numbers say more than the badge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gravel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Gas Snow Blowers on Gravel Driveways
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gravel driveways need a specific setup, and the wrong machine turns a snow blower into a stone thrower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Two-Stage</h3>

          <p>
            Only use a two-stage machine on gravel. A single-stage auger touches the ground by design and will pick up stones and throw them at windows, cars and people.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Raise the Skid Shoes</h3>

          <p>
            Set the skid shoes on a two-stage machine so the scraper bar rides about an inch above the gravel, and leave a thin layer of snow behind. The Husqvarna ST227 also lists an adjustable auger height for extra clearance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wait for a Base</h3>

          <p>
            Early in the season, let a base of packed snow form before you clear. Clearing down to loose gravel on the first storm guarantees stones in the auger.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. When to Buy a Gas Snow Blower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Timing affects both price and availability.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Before the First Big Storm</h3>

          <p>
            Popular models sell out after the first major storm of the season, and assembly and break-in take time. Buying in fall lets you assemble the machine, add oil and fresh fuel and test-start it before you need it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">End-of-Season Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            Late winter and early spring is when retailers clear stock, so it is a good time to buy if you can wait a season. Store a new machine as you would an old one: no untreated fuel in the tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check What Is in the Box</h3>

          <p>
            Gas snow blowers ship partly assembled and usually without fuel. Some include oil; check the listing and the manual, and never start the engine without the correct oil.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Clearing a Clogged Chute With Your Hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This causes most snow blower injuries. Shut the engine off, wait for everything to stop and use a clearing tool. The impeller can still move after the engine stops.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Untreated Gas in the Tank All Summer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stale fuel varnishes the carburetor and is the top reason a snow blower will not start in fall. Stabilize the fuel or run the tank dry, as your manual says.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Single-Stage Machine on Gravel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single-stage paddles touch the ground and throw stones. Use a two-stage machine with the skid shoes raised on gravel driveways.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Width and Ignoring Intake Height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wide machine with a low intake still struggles with deep drifts. Match the intake height to your usual snow depth, not just the width to your driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Engine in a Closed Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas engines produce carbon monoxide. Start the machine with the garage door open and move it outside promptly, and never warm it up with the door shut.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

