        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas snow blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Briggs & Stratton 26-inch two-stage is the best gas snow blower here: a 223cc engine, 26-inch path, 19-inch intake, electric start and lights, per its listing. Choose the 30-inch Briggs for long driveways, the Champion 301cc for deep snow, and the 22-inch single-stage Briggs for paved drives with moderate snow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gas or electric snow blower better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas is better for deep, wet or frequent snow, long driveways and gravel, because engines keep full power and refuel in seconds. Battery and corded machines are lighter, quieter and need no fuel or oil, which suits short paved driveways and lighter snowfalls. Our electric vs gas comparison covers the trade-off in more detail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size gas snow blower do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 22- to 24-inch machine suits a one-car driveway, a 26- to 27-inch machine suits most two-car driveways, and a 30-inch machine suits long, wide or shared driveways. Match the intake height to your usual snow depth: about 20 to 21 inches if drifts regularly reach your knees; a lower intake is fine for light snow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of gas do you put in a snow blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most snow blowers run on fresh regular unleaded gasoline; check the manual for the octane and maximum ethanol content allowed. Avoid fuel above the maker's ethanol limit, add stabilizer when you fill the can, and use fuel within about a month. Four-stroke snow blower engines take oil separately, never mixed into the gas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a single-stage gas snow blower worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for paved driveways and moderate snow. A single-stage gas machine is lighter, easier to store and cleans down to the pavement. It is the wrong choice for gravel, which it throws, and for heavy drifts or the plow ridge, where a two-stage machine's steel auger and impeller do far better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do gas snow blowers need electric start?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is not essential, but it is the most useful feature on a gas snow blower. Cold engines are hard to pull-start, and a 120-volt starter turns the engine over with a button from a household outlet. Every pick here has electric start, and the recoil cord remains as a backup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I leave gas in my snow blower over summer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Never leave untreated gas in the tank. Either run the tank dry or fill it with stabilized fuel and run the engine to circulate it, following your manual's recommendation. Stale fuel leaves varnish in the carburetor, which is the most common reason a snow blower will not start in fall.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

